Render the rule-only trait through traitDeclaration, so every generated rule trait derives DracoType (closes #50 item 2)

Dev accepted, in the words "Accepted, build it", the one next-priority recommendation brought from the reconciled backlog
of 24 open issues after the verification of 2026-11-08. OWNING ISSUE: https://github.com/ejb816/nexonix/issues/50,
"Generator: align rule traits with the root parent" — item 1 (the DracoType strip from the explicit derivation lists,
Dev's uniform-root decision of 2026-07-29) landed in b93e3a1 and eafc649; item 2, the November 4 status comment's
remaining scope ("DracoGenerator.spelledSource still emits a bare trait for the rule-only branch"), is this commit.
With it the issue has no remaining scope.

WHY. The rule-only branch of `DracoGenerator.spelledSource` hard-coded `trait $ruleName` while every other branch
(composed, actor, domain, object-only, leaf) spells its trait through `traitDeclaration`, which renders the modules
modifier, the header formals, the derivation (`typeExtends`, the rooted parent included) and the element body. The
companion beside it is rooted — `ruleGlobal` emits `object X extends App with DracoType` when the loaded chain reaches
DracoType — so of a rule definition's three projections two showed the root and the trait contradicted it.
`TypeLoader.rooted` has meant "absent derivation derives DracoType" for every definition since 2026-07-27; a rule was
the one kind whose trait did not say so.

MEASURED before recommending, on the tree at 7d4ae0a: the branch covers exactly 23 definitions, every rule-only type
in the corpus — the five draco validation rules (CollectProblems, Completeness, DerivationResolvable, SelfDeclaration,
Uniqueness), the three primes rules (AddNaturalSequence, PrimesFromNaturalSequence, RemoveCompositeNumbers),
gendrake.Emit and generator.EmissionReceived, the five editor rules (CreateHeld, DeleteUnheld, GenerateRequested,
ReadUnheld, UpdateUnheld) and the eight example rules (ConsumeReport and OriginateReport in aerial, ethereal, marine and
terrestrial). None carries elements, globals, header formals, a factory, a derivation or modules, so `traitDeclaration`
renders each as exactly one line, `trait X extends DracoType`; every one of the 23 files already imports `draco._`, as the
actor files that spell `trait Creator extends DracoType` do. Nothing instantiates a rule trait (no `new <RuleTrait>`), no
test pins a bare rule header (MinimalTypeTest's "expected a bare trait" is a stub type on the leaf path), and
`Rule[T]` has no bound, so this is a projection alignment, not a compile fix. The two other bare `trait X` lines under
src — draco.dreams.Service and domains.sentient.EgoActor — are hand-written scaffolds with no definition, untouched.

BUILT. One engine change: the rule branch calls `traitDeclaration(td)` in place of the literal, and the now-unused
`ruleName` binding goes with it. The 23 generated files change one line each, applied as the engine renders them;
the gates are the check. The TEST CORPUS follows the same way: the two forest scenario rules under
src/test/scala/scenario/forest (AshBirchAlarm, BirchAlarmReceived), pinned by ScenarioGenTest's "every scenario
definition projects to the Scala committed beside it", take the same line; and TupleFactReteTest, which writes
`org/nexonix/rules/rete/rules/TupleFact.scala` through the engine on every run, rewrote it with the line during the
direct run — 26 generated Scala files in all, one line each, the engine's output. The one other bare trait under
src/test, domains.natural.NaturalActor, has no definition and is untouched. No definition or JSON changes; no test
source changes other than those generated files; no test count or headline moves.

THE ENGINE-CHANGE PROTOCOL (DRACO.md §4): the compiled engine cannot regenerate its own change, so the engine was
recompiled with scalac into the direct-run overlay together with the 23 corpus files and the three test-tree files
(every file compiles with the new line), and `GeneratorCLI generate` from the recompiled classes was compared with
each committed file that has a definition on disk — 25 of 25 whitespace-identical (TupleFact's definition is built by
its test, which wrote the file itself). DracoGenTest pins the ten main rules, ExampleDomainsGenTest the eight example rules, EditorTest's
trio tests the five editor rules, GenerateAndCompileTest compiles the main corpus' output, and PrimesRulesTest writes
the three primes rules through the engine into src/main (the direct run left the tree with the intended changes only).

